  "codebook": {
    "definition": "Credit-claiming is defined as a senator's communication to constituents or key stakeholders asserting personal responsibility, involvement, or influence in securing a desirable government action, benefit, funding allocation, or legislative success.",
    "clarification": "To qualify as credit-claiming, a press release must meet at least one of the following criteria:\n1. The senator announces federal funds, grants, or projects awarded to their state or district, and explicitly associates their own name, office, or committee assignment with securing or supporting those funds.\n2. The senator claims to have written, introduced, sponsored, or successfully added an amendment or provision that was enacted into law or successfully passed through a critical legislative stage (e.g., committee passage).\n3. The senator highlights a personal intervention, letter, or meeting with a federal agency or executive official that directly led to a favorable policy change, disaster designation, or bureaucratic relief for their home-state constituents.",
    "positive_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "2318",
        "explanation": "The senator explicitly states: 'I helped secure an expedited environmental review from the federal government and facilitated the return of the project to the national priority list for federal funding,' linking his personal work directly to the local benefit."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "1862",
        "explanation": "The senators declare they 'have delivered on their promise to secure federal funding' and state they 'were proud to go to bat for these dollars,' showing active and successful efforts to obtain money for local projects."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "1257",
        "explanation": "The release announces a local grant and directly attributes it to Senator DeWine's personal legislative achievement, noting that the money was received 'as part of his Firefighter Investment and Response Enhancement (FIRE) Act, which Senator DeWine wrote and passed.'"
      }
    ],
    "negative_clarification": "Do not classify a text as credit-claiming if it meets any of the following criteria:\n1. The senator is merely expressing an opinion, taking a stance, or explaining a vote on a pending bill or policy issue (Position-taking).\n2. The senator is criticizing the President, another politician, or an executive agency without asserting any positive, personal role in achieving a successful outcome (Oversight/Criticism).\n3. The text is purely symbolic, such as a holiday message, a memorial tribute, or a condolence on the passing of a colleague.\n4. The text describes a pending advocacy effort, a meeting, or a call to action where no final, positive result has yet been achieved.",
    "negative_examples": [
      {
